{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ b690 ROOT-CAUSED, FIXED, PUSHED AS GERRIT 1178 — and the fix was NOT in any suspected seam ═══\n\n**THE ROOT CAUSE, proven by experiment, not inference.** `PydanticAIRunner.run` opened with\n`cfg = self._config` (`runner.py:239`) and resolved the model from it (`:283 resolved =\n_pai_model(cfg)`), DISCARDING `req.config.model`. So `RunnerAgentStep` computed each step's declared\nclass correctly and the runner threw it away one layer lower. Experiment: build the runner with\n`model=\"CONSTRUCTOR-MODEL\"`, call `run()` with `req.config.model=\"PER-STEP-MODEL\"`, spy `_pai_model`\n→ it received `CONSTRUCTOR-MODEL`. Post-fix the same probe prints `PER-STEP-MODEL`.\n\n**THE FIX** is `cfg = _effective_config(self._config, req)` plus a documented helper that substitutes\nthe per-call model into a COPY of the config. It is not a new mechanism: `build_usage_limits` already\nstates \"`self._config` (`cfg`) is the floor; `req.config` is the per-call override\" (bug 59bc, so a\nfinding-rich verifier could raise its own budget without mutating a shared runner), and `temperature`\nfollows the same rule at the same call. The model was the lone outlier. Substituted into the CONFIG\nrather than read at the resolution point so the ProviderSession, capability record, fallback chain\nand cache settings all follow the model that actually runs.\n\n**TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE PRIOR INVESTIGATION — both were keeping the search away from the seam.**\n 1. **H3 was eliminated on unsound reasoning.** \"Empty table ⇒ standard=SONNET, but the log showed\n    OPUS\" ignores `_parse_slot`'s middle term: `model = raw.get(\"model\") or fallback_default or\n    _DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_CLASS[name]`, where `fallback_default` is the deprecated bare\n    `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`. With that set, an unconfigured class resolves to it for all three classes.\n    (It is unset on this host, so it was not the live mechanism — but the inference was invalid.)\n 2. **MEASURED: this repo configures NO `model_classes` table**, so `frontier` resolves to the\n    built-in opus default — which IS `cfg.model`. So `base`/`round_a`/`round_b`, all declaring\n    `frontier`, were EXPECTED to run on opus whether honoured or not. Six-plus of the nine live rows\n    carried no information; only `verify`/`coach` (declaring `standard` → sonnet) ever discriminated.\n    Any future probe MUST configure a table that differs from cfg.model or it proves nothing.\n\n**THE SAME ROOT CAUSE WAS BREAKING FOUR MORE PER-CALL MODEL CHOICES, ALL IN PLAN-REVIEW.** Audited\nall 22 `RunRequest` sites: four pass a config different from the runner they were built with, and all\nfour were silently ineffective — `xcheck.py:199` and `:337` and `plan_review/__init__.py:164` (three\n`_verifier_cfg` sub-calls meant to run on the cheaper decisive model, in fact running on frontier),\nand `prerequisites.py:228`. The last is worse than a missed optimisation: it is a SIZE-ESCALATION\nLADDER whose own comment says \"a ladder escalation re-runs a bin on a HIGHER model … must report what\nactually ran, not what was configured (client report §4)\". Both halves were broken — the escalation\nnever escalated, and it recorded the model it ASKED for. **So the fix changes plan-review gate\nbehaviour** (verifier passes frontier → the verifier model; the prerequisite ladder starts actually\nescalating). Intended in every case, but operator-visible; flagged on b690 rather than decided, and\nNOT narrowed to code-review, because narrowing would knowingly leave four defects in place.\n\n**TESTS — and a test-design lesson worth carrying.** The capture point named in b690's own plan\n(`req.config.model` at `runner.run(req)`) is ONE LAYER TOO HIGH to see this defect: it observes the\nvalue handed in, and the bug is that the runner ignores what it is handed. That test\n(`test_code_review_step_model_attribution.py`) is GREEN pre-fix and worth keeping — it pins the upper\nhalf and is mutation-checked — but the RED evidence had to come from beneath model resolution:\n * `tests/unit/test_llm_model_per_request.py` — spies `_pai_model`; 2 failed / 1 passed pre-fix, the\n   passing one being the control, so the failures cannot be \"no model reached the resolver\".\n * `tests/unit/workflow/test_code_review_usage_attribution.py` — the real gate doc through the real\n   interpreter and a real `PydanticAIRunner` (model replaced by a `FunctionModel`), asserting all five\n   steps resolve their declared class AND the usage log's per-step attribution, from a log the run\n   produced. RED pre-fix naming all five steps.\n * `tests/unit/test_usage_log_attribution.py` — the writer contract: omission-not-null, the\n   `CLASS_NAMES` filter, ContextVar scoping incl. nesting and the raising-step case, `summarize()`\n   still folding the new shape, and inertness when `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` is unset.\n\n**STEP 0 SHIPPED AND WORKS.** `usage_log` gained a ContextVar (`step_identity`/`active_step`) bound by\nthe interpreter around every leaf AND batch step, plus `step` + `model_class` on `record()`.\nA ContextVar over a `RunRequest` field because `RunRequest` is built at 25 sites, most with no step.\nMEASURED CORRECTION to the plan's premise: the batch runner dispatches each overlay under the\nOVERLAY's own prompt, not the `batch.prompt` finder — so `op` is an overlay name and the real\nambiguity is round_a-vs-round_b (both blocks declare the same overlay list), not base-vs-finder.\n\n**STATE:** commit `2e17171e5c` on `feat/llm-provider-seam` (on top of 44d9250ea) → **Gerrit 1178**,\nNEW, no votes yet. Lint + typecheck clean; a full unit run WITH the fix was 6394 passed / 19 skipped;\naffected areas 596 passed. NOTHING SUBMITTED (epic 061c still open).\nDocs: `docs/llm-framework.md` now documents the two new usage-log fields.\n\n**EPIC CHILD INVENTORY (the handoff undercounted — it listed the blocked chain and 5e75 only).**\n16 children: 7 CLOSED (2932, 343b, 7468, 9249, dc0d, eb58, f184); 3 IN_PROGRESS (3768, 55bc, b690);\n6 OPEN — READY: **5e75** (task, ADR 0056 extraction), **7a79** (bug, `warn_if_cache_ineffective`\nignores the 4096-token cache floor), **baa8** (bug, live LLM execution does not run in the external\ntier); BLOCKED: 0d76 → eb6e → 8625. Note `55bc` (bug, LLM sidecar retention divergence) is claimed\nand was not in the previous handoff.\nThe epic's own plan-review shows `pin_status: stale-pin-drift` (0d76's child fingerprint moved) —\nrelevant when the epic itself is closed.\n\n**IN FLIGHT:** a live `rebar review-code` on this very change with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` set and a\nDISCRIMINATING table (`cfg.model`=opus, frontier=sonnet, standard=haiku — so ANY opus row proves a\ndropped declaration). Needs `REBAR_VERIFY_ENABLE_CODE_REVIEW=1`; the gate is off by default and the\nfirst attempt returned `code-review-disabled` with an empty verdict. That run is the evidence for\nb690's second [operator-attested] criterion and for 3768's.\n**b690's FIRST [operator-attested] criterion cannot now be met as written** — it asks for the live\nprobe to be re-run on the BROKEN code to name the seam; the seam was instead named by a deterministic\nlocal experiment (stronger: isolates the mechanism, no spend, replayable) and the code is fixed.\nFlagged for the operator rather than quietly counted as met."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785622777129291001,"uuid":"39f2de0a-61d5-4e6c-ab97-bbfa3c534032"}